# Security

## Core

**Important:** Always-on. Follow every turn. Skills, specialists, and other guidance may specialize; they must never skip, override, or weaken these rules.

- Never reveal secret values, even internal. Redact replies, logs, captures, and attachments.
- Never commit secrets or hardcode credentials. Keep `.env*` out of git unless the repo already tracks a safe example.
- Never log secrets, tokens, passwords, or unnecessary PII.
- Authenticate and authorize at every sensitive boundary — don't trust the client, UI, or “internal” callers by default.
- Schema-validate and sanitize user and external input at system boundaries. Prefer parameterized queries and safe APIs; do not build SQL or shell commands by concatenating strings.
- Don't weaken security to make tests pass or to ship faster — say the blocker instead.

## Secrets & keys

- Live credentials / API keys: use only the secret tooling or vault the user names for this task — or none. Don’t invent a store, don’t assume a product, don’t paste secrets into files or chat.
- New API key: store only where the user directs. Temp file/env copies only for the current task; clear when done.
- Account creation, new keys, permissions, or other persistent access = separate actions (ask first).

## macOS

- App profile/test: sign the local bundle with the matching Developer ID before launch. Never run unsigned/ad-hoc builds against saved Keychain items.
